# Rust
target/
test_snapshots/

# Deployment artefacts, regenerated by `make demo` and `make coverage`. The env file, the JSON
# state dumps and the lcov data are build outputs.
#
# `evidence.md` and `coverage.txt` are the exceptions, and they are committed on purpose: they ARE
# the deliverable. A reviewer opening the repository has to find the transaction hashes without
# running anything, and a deliverable that only exists on the machine that produced it is not
# evidence of anything.
#
# The contents are excluded rather than the directory. `.deploy/` would exclude the directory
# itself, so git never descends into it and a negation underneath is dead — which is exactly what
# happened in the repository this was split out of: the file the comment above calls the
# deliverable could not be committed at all. Excluding `.deploy/*` leaves the directory visible,
# so the two negations below are reachable.
.deploy/*
!.deploy/evidence.md
!.deploy/coverage.txt

# Secrets and local overrides. No secret belongs in this repository at all — identities live in
# the Stellar CLI keystore and `scripts/deploy.sh` writes addresses only — but the rule stays as
# a backstop.
.env
.env.*

# Logs and scratch
*.log
.DS_Store
